var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#CommunityEcology.jl","page":"Home","text":"CommunityEcology.jl analyses community count tables in Julia. Samples are rows and features are columns. A Community also holds sample metadata and can include taxonomy, sequences, and a phylogenetic tree.","title":"CommunityEcology.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Create-and-analyse-a-community","page":"Home","text":"Install with Julia's package manager (] add CommunityEcology) after registration. Until then, add the repository URL and the NonmetricMDS.jl dependency.\n\nusing CommunityEcology, DataFrames\n\ncounts = [8 1 0; 7 2 1; 0 1 9; 1 0 8]\nmeta = DataFrame(sample=[\"A1\", \"A2\", \"B1\", \"B2\"], site=[\"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"B\"])\nc = Community(counts; samples=meta.sample,\n              features=[\"oak\", \"pine\", \"birch\"], metadata=meta)\n\nalpha_diversity(c)                 # one row per sample\nd = beta_diversity(c, :braycurtis) # pairwise dissimilarities\nordinate(d, c, :pcoa)              # principal coordinates\n\nFor a multisite Jaccard decomposition, use beta_partition(c) or group by a metadata column:\n\nbeta_partition(c; family=:jaccard)\nbeta_partition(c, :site; family=:sorensen)\n\nThe decomposition returns total dissimilarity, turnover, and nestedness. The grouped result also reports within-group dispersion. Each group needs at least two samples.","title":"Create and analyse a community"},{"category":"section","location":"#Main-workflows","page":"Home","text":"alpha_diversity, rarefy, and specaccum describe within-sample diversity and accumulation.\nbeta_diversity, ordinate, and rda analyse differences among samples.\npermanova, betadisper, mantel, and envfit test associations and group differences.\nrelative, hellinger, and presence_absence transform counts into new Community values.\nwrite_community and read_community round-trip a community through files.\n\nnmds and pcoa are provided by NonmetricMDS.jl; PERMANOVA and dispersion methods likewise use separate packages. Load a Makie backend to enable the optional plotting recipes. The README has a longer example and describes differences from R's vegan package.","title":"Main workflows"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-reference","page":"Home","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"module","location":"#CommunityEcology.CommunityEcology","page":"Home","text":"CommunityEcology\n\nCommunity data for ecologists: counts, diversity, beta diversity, ordination, phylogenetic analyses, PERMANOVA, and tests of dispersion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.CommunityEcology"},{"category":"type","location":"#CommunityEcology.BetaPartition","page":"Home","text":"BetaPartition\n\nThe table returned by beta_partition(c, groups...): one row per group of samples, with the multi-site β-diversity partition and, optionally, the dispersion of the group. A Tables.jl table (DataFrame(bp) gives the values); it displays rounded to bp.digits places, as plain text, HTML or Markdown (so it renders as a table in Quarto documents, Word included).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.BetaPartition"},{"category":"type","location":"#CommunityEcology.Community","page":"Home","text":"Community\n\nA community data set: a table of counts with one row per sample and one column per feature (ASV, OTU or taxon), with optional feature sequences, a taxonomy (one row per feature, one column per rank), sample metadata (one row per sample, first column sample) and a phylogenetic tree.\n\nConstruct one from a sequence table (DenoiseDNA's SequenceTable), from a count matrix, or read one saved with write_community:\n\nc = Community(seqtab; taxonomy=taxa, metadata=\"samples.csv\")\nc = Community(counts; samples=names, features=ids)\n\nIndex it like a matrix, by position, Bool vector or name: c[1:5, :], c[:, [\"ASV1\", \"ASV2\"]], c[c.metadata.site .== \"A\", :].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.Community"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.Community-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"Home","text":"Community(counts; samples, features, sequences=String[], taxonomy=nothing,\n          metadata=nothing, tree=nothing, by=:sample)\nCommunity(seqtab; taxonomy=nothing, metadata=nothing, tree=nothing, prefix=\"ASV\", by=:sample)\n\ncounts is a samples × features matrix. A sequence table from DenoiseDNA (anything with samples, sequences and table fields) gives the counts, sample names and sequences; features are named ASV1, ASV2, … in table order.\n\ntaxonomy: any table with one row per feature (e.g. the TaxonomyTable from AssignTaxonomy). If it has a Sequence column, rows are matched to features by sequence; otherwise they must be in feature order.\nmetadata: a table or a CSV/TSV file with one row per sample, matched by the column by (default :sample). Samples without metadata get missing.\ntree: a PhyloTree whose tips are the features.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.Community"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.Community-Tuple{DataFrames.AbstractDataFrame}","page":"Home","text":"Community(table; sample=:sample, features=nothing, metadata=nothing, kw...)\n\nA community from a wide table (a DataFrame, CSV file, or any Tables.jl table) with one row per sample: the column sample names the samples and the features columns (default: every other column) hold the counts.\n\nc = Community(\"counts.csv\"; metadata=\"samples.csv\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.Community"},{"category":"type","location":"#CommunityEcology.DistanceMatrix","page":"Home","text":"DistanceMatrix\n\nA symmetric matrix of dissimilarities between samples, with their labels and the method used. It is an AbstractMatrix, so it can be passed to permanova, betadisper, nmds or pcoa directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.DistanceMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"#CommunityEcology.MantelResult","page":"Home","text":"MantelResult\n\nResult of mantel: correlation r, permutation pvalue, number of permutations, correlation method, and test alternative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.MantelResult"},{"category":"type","location":"#CommunityEcology.Ordination","page":"Home","text":"Ordination\n\nSample coordinates from ordinate, with the sample metadata. Fields: points (samples × axes), samples, metadata, method (:nmds or :pcoa), distance, axislabels, and the underlying result (an NMDSResult or PCoAResult).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.Ordination"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.Ordination-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}, DataFrames.DataFrame}","page":"Home","text":"Ordination(points, metadata; method=:nmds, distance=:unknown, axislabels=[\"Dim 1\", \"Dim 2\", …])\n\nAn Ordination from saved coordinates (samples × axes) and their metadata, e.g. to plot an ordination written to disk. The sample names are metadata.sample when there is such a column, otherwise \"1\", \"2\", ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.Ordination"},{"category":"type","location":"#CommunityEcology.PhyloTree","page":"Home","text":"PhyloTree\n\nA rooted tree. Tips are nodes 1:ntips(t), named tiplabels(t). Fields: parent (0 at the root), edge (length of the branch above each node), children, names (tip labels; internal nodes have \"\"), root.\n\nBuild one with build_tree (from sequences) or nj_tree (from a distance matrix), or read one with read_newick.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.PhyloTree"},{"category":"type","location":"#CommunityEcology.RDAResult","page":"Home","text":"RDAResult\n\nConstrained redundancy analysis from rda. sites and features are score matrices (rows are samples and features respectively), eigenvalues are constrained eigenvalues, explained gives each axis' share of total response inertia, and r2/adjusted_r2 summarize the full fitted model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.RDAResult"},{"category":"method","location":"#BetaDispersion.betadisper-Tuple{Community, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"betadisper(c::Community, variable::Symbol; distance=:braycurtis, kw...)\n\nDistances of samples to their group's spatial median, grouping by the metadata column variable. Test with permutest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BetaDispersion.betadisper"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.add_metadata-Tuple{Community, Any}","page":"Home","text":"add_metadata(c, meta; by=:sample)\n\nA copy of c with sample metadata from meta (a table or a CSV/TSV file), replacing any it had.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.add_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.add_taxonomy-Tuple{Community, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"add_taxonomy(c, reference::AbstractString; kw...)\n\nClassify the community's sequences against a DADA2-formatted reference file with AssignTaxonomy's naive Bayesian classifier and attach the ranks; keywords go to AssignTaxonomy.assignTaxonomy. Needs using AssignTaxonomy. Pipes well:\n\n@chain seqtab begin\n    Community\n    add_taxonomy(\"silva_nr99_v138.2_toGenus_trainset.fa.gz\")\n    collapse(:Family)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.add_taxonomy"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.add_taxonomy-Tuple{Community, Any}","page":"Home","text":"add_taxonomy(c, taxonomy)\n\nA copy of c with a new taxonomy: any table with one row per feature, such as AssignTaxonomy's result. Rows are matched to features by a Sequence column when there is one, otherwise taken in feature order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.add_taxonomy"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.add_tree-Tuple{Community, Any}","page":"Home","text":"add_tree(c, tree)\n\nA copy of c with a phylogenetic tree: a PhyloTree, Newick text, or a Newick file. Every feature must be a tip; other tips are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.add_tree"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.alpha_diversity-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"alpha_diversity(c; measures=[:richness, :shannon, :simpson, :invsimpson, :chao1, :pielou],\n                metadata=true)\n\nA DataFrame with one row per sample: sample, depth, the requested measures and (with metadata=true) the sample metadata, ready for plotting or modelling. With a tree, :faith_pd is also available.\n\nRichness depends on sequencing depth: compare it across samples after rarefy, or look at rarecurve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.alpha_diversity"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.beta_diversity","page":"Home","text":"beta_diversity(c, method=:braycurtis; pseudocount=0.5)\n\nDissimilarities between the samples of c:\n\n:braycurtis: Bray-Curtis on counts, Σ|xᵢ - yᵢ| / Σ(xᵢ + yᵢ) (use relative(c) first to weight samples equally)\n:jaccard, :sorensen: on presence/absence\n:ruzicka: quantitative Jaccard (as vegan::vegdist(method=\"jaccard\"))\n:manhattan, :canberra (mean over non-double-zero features), :gower (feature-range-scaled mean absolute difference)\n:euclidean, :chord, :hellinger (Euclidean on transformed data), :aitchison (Euclidean on the centred log-ratio, with pseudocount)\n:unifrac, :wunifrac: unweighted and weighted (normalised) UniFrac (Lozupone & Knight 2005; Lozupone et al. 2007), which need a tree\n\nSamples with no reads are rejected: drop them with prune.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.beta_diversity"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.beta_partition-Tuple{Community, Symbol, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Home","text":"beta_partition(c, groups::Symbol...; family=:jaccard, dispersion=:braycurtis, digits=2)\n\nThe multi-site β-diversity partition (see beta_partition(c)) for each group of samples, the groups being the combinations of the metadata columns groups, in order of appearance. With dispersion set to a dissimilarity (see beta_diversity), each group's dispersion is added: the mean distance of its samples to the group centroid in principal coordinate space (betadisper with type=:centroid), computed on c as given; pass relative(c) for dispersion of proportions. dispersion=nothing leaves it out.\n\nbeta_partition(relative(c), :transfer, :carbon)   # one row per transfer × carbon source\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.beta_partition"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.beta_partition-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"beta_partition(c; family=:jaccard)\n\nMulti-site β-diversity of the samples of c, from presence/absence, and its partition into species turnover and nestedness (Baselga 2010, 2012; as betapart::beta.multi). With Sᵢ the richness of sample i, S_T the total richness, and bᵢⱼ the number of species in i but not j, summed over pairs of samples as min = Σ min(bᵢⱼ, bⱼᵢ) and max = Σ max(bᵢⱼ, bⱼᵢ), and a = Σ Sᵢ - S_T:\n\nfamily=:jaccard: total βJAC = (min + max) / (a + min + max), turnover βJTU = 2min / (a + 2min), nestedness βJNE = βJAC - βJTU;\nfamily=:sorensen: βSOR = (min + max) / (2a + min + max), βSIM = min / (a + min), βSNE = βSOR - βSIM.\n\nReturns (total, turnover, nestedness, relative_turnover), the last being turnover / total. For one table per group of samples, see beta_partition(c, groups...).\n\nbeta_partition(dune())          # (total = 0.88…, turnover = 0.81…, nestedness = 0.07…, …)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.beta_partition"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.build_tree-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"build_tree(c::Community; band=32)\nbuild_tree(seqs; labels, band=32)\n\nA neighbour-joining tree (rooted at its midpoint) of the feature sequences of c, from Jukes-Cantor distances of pairwise alignments; returns a copy of c with the tree attached, ready for UniFrac and Faith's PD. Given sequences, returns the PhyloTree.\n\nThis is a quick distance tree, good enough for UniFrac and for a picture. For a phylogeny to interpret on its own, align with a multiple aligner and build a maximum-likelihood tree; read_newick reads the result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.build_tree"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.chao1-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"chao1(x)\n\nBias-corrected Chao1 estimate of richness, S + F₁(F₁ - 1) / (2(F₂ + 1)), from integer counts (F₁ singletons, F₂ doubletons), as vegan::estimateR. Denoised ASV tables contain few singletons, which makes Chao1 close to the observed richness there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.chao1"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.chord-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"chord(c)\n\nScale each sample to Euclidean length one. Euclidean distances on this transformation are chord distances. Empty samples remain zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.chord"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.clr-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"clr(c; pseudocount=0.5)\n\nCentred log-ratio transformation, log(x + pseudocount) minus the sample's mean log. Euclidean distances on it are Aitchison distances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.clr"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.collapse-Tuple{Community, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"collapse(c, rank::Symbol)\n\nSum the features that share a taxon at rank (e.g. :Genus). A feature not assigned at that rank is grouped as \"unclassified X\", where X is its deepest assigned rank above. The result's features are the taxon names and its taxonomy keeps the ranks down to rank. Sequences and tree are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.collapse"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.cophenetic-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"cophenetic(t::PhyloTree)\ncophenetic(c::Community)\n\nPatristic (tip-to-tip) distances along the branches of a tree. For a community, rows and columns follow features(c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.cophenetic"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.dune-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"dune()\n\nVegetation of 20 dune meadows on Terschelling, the Netherlands (Jongman, ter Braak & van Tongeren 1987): cover class (0–9) of 30 plant species, with metadata A1 (thickness of the A1 soil horizon, cm), Moisture (1, 2, 4 or 5), Management (\"BF\" biological farming, \"HF\" hobby farming, \"NM\" nature conservation, \"SF\" standard farming), Use (\"Hayfield\", \"Haypastu\", \"Pasture\") and Manure (0–4). The taxonomy gives each species' genus, family, order, superorder and subclass.\n\nThe same data as dune, dune.env and dune.taxon in R's vegan package.\n\nc = dune()\npermanova(c, @formula(1 ~ Management))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.dune"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.envfit-Tuple{Ordination}","page":"Home","text":"envfit(o::Ordination; variables=nothing, permutations=999,\n       rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nFit sample metadata to an ordination, in the style of vegan::envfit. Numeric variables use vector fitting; categorical variables use factor centroids. Returns a DataFrame with variable, level (missing for numeric vectors), squared correlation r2, permutation pvalue, and axis1, axis2, etc. Numeric coordinates are unit vector directions; factor coordinates are level centroids. Missing values are omitted per variable. variables=nothing uses all metadata columns other than sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.envfit"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.factorfit-Tuple{Ordination, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"factorfit(o::Ordination, variable::Symbol; permutations=999,\n          rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nFit a categorical metadata variable to an ordination. Returns level centroids with a shared between-group R² and permutation p-value. The result has the same columns as envfit, so results can be combined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.factorfit"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.faith_pd-Tuple{CommunityEcology.PhyloTree, AbstractVector{Bool}}","page":"Home","text":"faith_pd(t::PhyloTree, present)\n\nFaith's phylogenetic diversity: total length of the branches joining the root to the tips in present (a Bool vector over the tips, or tip names).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.faith_pd"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.features-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"features(c) -> Vector{String}\n\nFeature (ASV/taxon) names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.features"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.filter_features-Tuple{Any, Community}","page":"Home","text":"filter_features(f, c)\nfilter_features(c, rank => predicate)\n\nKeep features whose taxonomy row satisfies f(row), e.g. filter_features(c, :Kingdom => ==(\"Bacteria\")). Missing ranks never match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.filter_features"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.filter_samples-Tuple{Any, Community}","page":"Home","text":"filter_samples(f, c)\nfilter_samples(c, column => predicate)\n\nKeep the samples whose metadata row satisfies f(row), e.g. filter_samples(r -> r.day > 10, c) or filter_samples(c, :site => ==(\"A\")).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.filter_samples"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.hellinger-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"hellinger(c)\n\nThe Hellinger transformation: square roots of relative abundances (Legendre & Gallagher 2001). Euclidean distances on it are Hellinger distances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.hellinger"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.hill-Tuple{AbstractVector, Real}","page":"Home","text":"hill(x, q)\n\nHill number of order q, the effective number of features: q = 0 richness, q = 1 exp(Shannon), q = 2 inverse Simpson.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.hill"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.invsimpson-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"invsimpson(x) -> Float64\n\nInverse Simpson index 1 / Σ pᵢ²; 0.0 for an empty sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.invsimpson"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.keep_tips-Tuple{CommunityEcology.PhyloTree, Any}","page":"Home","text":"keep_tips(t::PhyloTree, names)\n\nThe subtree spanned by the tips names, with unary nodes removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.keep_tips"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.lump_rare","page":"Home","text":"lump_rare(c, n=10; other=\"Other\")\n\nKeep the n most abundant features (see top_features) and sum the rest into one feature named other. Handy before a composition plot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.lump_rare"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.mantel-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Home","text":"mantel(a, b; method=:pearson, alternative=:greater, permutations=999,\n       rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nMantel test of association between two distance matrices with the same sample order. method=:spearman correlates average ranks (including ties). The permutation test relabels the rows and columns of b together. The p-value includes the observed arrangement, so its minimum is 1/(permutations+1). Use alternative=:less or :two_sided for other tails.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.mantel"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.midpoint_root-Tuple{CommunityEcology.PhyloTree}","page":"Home","text":"midpoint_root(t::PhyloTree)\n\nRe-root t at the midpoint of its longest tip-to-tip path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.midpoint_root"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.miseq_sop-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"miseq_sop()\n\nThe mouse gut samples of the mothur MiSeq SOP (miseq_sop_reads), processed into a community: 19 samples × the amplicon sequence variants (ASVs) that DenoiseDNA infers from the reads, with their sequences, SILVA 138.2 taxonomy (AssignTaxonomy), a neighbour-joining tree (MSABuilder and PhyloBuilder), and metadata day (days post weaning) and time (\"Early\", days 0–9; \"Late\", days 141–150). The mock community is left out.\n\ndata/miseq_sop/build.jl in this package is the script that made it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.miseq_sop"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.miseq_sop_reads","page":"Home","text":"miseq_sop_reads(dir=\"MiSeq_SOP\")\n\nPaired-end 16S rRNA (V4) MiSeq reads from the mothur MiSeq SOP (Kozich et al. 2013): faecal samples from one mouse on days 0–9 and 141–150 after weaning, plus a mock community of 20 known strains (\"Mock\"). The 35 MB archive is downloaded and unpacked into dir the first time; later calls only list the files.\n\nReturns a DataFrame with one row per sample: sample, and the paths of the forward and reverse FASTQ files.\n\nreads = miseq_sop_reads()\nfilterAndTrim(reads.forward, filtF, reads.reverse, filtR; truncLen=(240, 160))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.miseq_sop_reads"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.nfeatures-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"nfeatures(c) -> Int\n\nNumber of features (columns) in c.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.nfeatures"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.nj_tree-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"nj_tree(D; labels=string.(1:n), nonnegative=true)\n\nNeighbour-joining tree (Saitou & Nei 1987; the algorithm of Studier & Keppler 1988, as ape::nj) of the symmetric distance matrix D, rooted at its midpoint. Negative branch lengths, which NJ can produce, are set to zero unless nonnegative=false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.nj_tree"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.ordinate","page":"Home","text":"ordinate(c, method=:nmds; distance=:braycurtis, k=2, kw...)\nordinate(D::DistanceMatrix, c, method=:nmds; k=2, kw...)\n\nOrdinate the samples of c: non-metric MDS (:nmds, several random starts; keywords go to nmds) or principal coordinates (:pcoa), on the dissimilarity distance (see beta_diversity) or a given DistanceMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.ordinate"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.pielou-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"pielou(x) -> Float64\n\nPielou's evenness shannon(x) / log(richness(x)); NaN if richness ≤ 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.pielou"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_alpha","page":"Home","text":"plot_alpha(c::Community; measure=:shannon, group=nothing, size=(760, 470), markersize=11) -> Figure\nplot_alpha!(axis, c::Community; kwargs...)\n\nPlot one alpha_diversity measure per sample, or jittered by metadata column group with group means.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_alpha"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_composition","page":"Home","text":"plot_composition(c::Community; top, proportions, size, kwargs...) -> Figure\nplot_composition!(axis, c::Community; kwargs...)\n\nPlot the composition of each sample as stacked bars. Load a Makie backend first. Keywords:\n\ntop=10: plot the top most abundant features and lump the rest, unless features names the features to plot, in order; colors colours them.\nproportions=true: relative abundances (over all features) rather than counts.\nsortby: order the samples by metadata columns, each column or column => level order, outermost first (stable, so the original order is kept within groups).\nabove, below: metadata columns whose runs of consecutive samples get labelled brackets above or below the bars; grouplabels and groupcolors are Dicts from a group value to its label and bracket colour.\nbarwidth, bracketwidth, fontsize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_composition"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_dispersion","page":"Home","text":"plot_dispersion(bd::BetaDisper; size=(700, 450), markersize=11) -> Figure\nplot_dispersion!(axis, bd::BetaDisper; kwargs...)\n\nPlot each sample's distance to its group centre, with group means.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_dispersion"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_heatmap","page":"Home","text":"plot_heatmap(c::Community; proportions=true, colormap=:viridis, size=(850, 550)) -> Figure\nplot_heatmap!(axis, c::Community; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a samples × features heatmap of relative abundances (proportions=true) or counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_heatmap"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_ordination","page":"Home","text":"plot_ordination(o::Ordination; colorby, labels, size, kwargs...) -> Figure\nplot_ordination!(axis, o::Ordination; kwargs...)\n\nPlot the sample scores of o. Load a Makie backend first. Keywords:\n\ncolorby: metadata column for the colour (a colormap if numeric).\nmarkerby: metadata column for the marker.\npalette, markers: colours and markers, as vectors (matched to the sorted levels) or Dicts from level to colour or marker.\nalpha: opacity of the sample points; markersize; labels (sample names).\ncentroids=true (needs a categorical colorby): a centroid for each colorby group (centroidmarker, centroidsize), joined by dashed lines to the centroid of each of its markerby subgroups or, without markerby, to each of its points.\n\nTo plot saved coordinates, wrap them in Ordination(points, metadata).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_ordination"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_rarefaction","page":"Home","text":"plot_rarefaction(c::Community; step=nothing, linewidth=2, size=(760, 500)) -> Figure\nplot_rarefaction!(axis, c::Community; kwargs...)\n\nPlot rarecurve expected richness against depth, one line per sample. Legend shown for ≤ 12 samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_rarefaction"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_rda","page":"Home","text":"plot_rda(r::RDAResult; size=(800, 550), labels=false, showfeatures=true, markersize=11) -> Figure\nplot_rda!(axis, r::RDAResult; kwargs...)\n\nPlot RDA site scores on the first two constrained axes, with feature arrows if showfeatures. labels adds sample names. Needs two constrained axes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_rda"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_shepard","page":"Home","text":"plot_shepard(r; size=(700, 480), markersize=5) -> Figure\nplot_shepard!(axis, r; kwargs...)\n\nShepard diagram (ordination distance vs observed dissimilarity, with the fitted monotone curve). r is an NMDSResult or an NMDS Ordination.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_shepard"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.plot_tree","page":"Home","text":"plot_tree(t; size=(800, 550), linecolor=:teal, linewidth=2, labels=true) -> Figure\nplot_tree!(axis, t; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a rooted PhyloTree as a rectangular cladogram with branch lengths. t may be a Community with a tree. labels shows tip names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.plot_tree"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.presence_absence-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"presence_absence(c)\n\n1 where a feature was seen in a sample, 0 otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.presence_absence"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.prune-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"prune(c; min_count=1, min_samples=1, min_depth=1)\n\nDrop features seen fewer than min_count times in total or in fewer than min_samples samples, then samples with fewer than min_depth reads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.prune"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.ranks-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"ranks(c) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nTaxonomic rank columns; empty without taxonomy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.ranks"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.rarecurve-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"rarecurve(c; step=nothing, depths=nothing)\n\nRarefaction curves: the expected number of features in a random subsample of each depth (Hurlbert 1971; vegan::rarefy), computed exactly rather than by resampling. Returns a long DataFrame with sample, depth and richness, plus the sample metadata. step defaults to 1/50 of the largest depth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.rarecurve"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.rarefy","page":"Home","text":"rarefy(c, depth=minimum(sample_depths(c)); rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nSubsample every sample to depth reads without replacement. Samples with fewer reads are dropped (with a message), and features left with no reads are removed. Rarefying discards data; use it for richness comparisons or when a method requires equal depths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.rarefy"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.rda-Tuple{Community, AbstractVector{Symbol}}","page":"Home","text":"rda(c::Community, predictors; transform=:hellinger, k=2)\n\nRedundancy analysis of a community table constrained by numeric or categorical sample metadata columns. The response is Hellinger transformed by default; :relative and :raw are also available. Numeric predictors are centred; categorical predictors use reference-level dummy coding. The result contains fitted sample coordinates (sites), feature loadings (feature_scores), constrained eigenvalues, and adjusted R². Permutation inference for a model can be performed separately with permanova.\n\nThis is a basic RDA with no conditioning terms, weights, or formula interface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.rda"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.read_community-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"read_community(dir)\n\nRead a community saved with write_community.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.read_community"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.read_metadata-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"read_metadata(file; delim=nothing)\n\nRead a CSV or TSV sample sheet into a DataFrame (the delimiter is detected unless given).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.read_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.read_newick-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"read_newick(text_or_file)\n\nRead a tree in Newick format (from a string that starts with (, or a file).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.read_newick"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.relative-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"relative(c)\n\nCounts as proportions of each sample's total.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.relative"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.richness-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"richness(x) -> Int\n\nNumber of entries of x greater than zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.richness"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.sample_depths-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"sample_depths(c) -> Vector\n\nTotal count (sequencing depth) of each sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.sample_depths"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.samples-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"samples(c) -> Vector{String}\n\nSample names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.samples"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.sequence_distances-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"sequence_distances(seqs; band=32, model=:jc69)\n\nPairwise evolutionary distances between DNA sequences (strings or anything that converts to one), from banded global alignments with free end gaps. model=:jc69 applies the Jukes-Cantor correction to the proportion of differing aligned sites (gaps count as differences); :p returns the raw proportion. Runs on all Julia threads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.sequence_distances"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.sequences-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"sequences(c) -> Vector{String}\n\nFeature sequences; empty if unknown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.sequences"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.shannon-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"shannon(x) -> Float64\n\nShannon index -Σ pᵢ log pᵢ (natural log), as vegan::diversity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.shannon"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.simper-Tuple{Community, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"simper(c::Community, variable::Symbol; relative=false)\n\nBreak down mean between-group Bray–Curtis dissimilarity into contributions from individual features. Returns a DataFrame for every pair of levels in the metadata column variable, ordered by decreasing mean contribution. average is the mean contribution on the Bray–Curtis scale; fraction and cumulative divide by the total mean dissimilarity. mean_a and mean_b are group mean abundances. relative=true first converts each sample to relative abundance. Missing group labels are omitted.\n\nSIMPER is descriptive: a high contribution can reflect abundant, variable features even without a consistent group difference. Use permanova for a group-level permutation test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.simper"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.simpson-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Home","text":"simpson(x) -> Float64\n\nGini-Simpson index 1 - Σ pᵢ², as vegan::diversity(index=\"simpson\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.simpson"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.specaccum-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"specaccum(c::Community)\n\nExpected number of features observed after pooling 1 through nsamples(c) samples in random order. This is the exact, combinatorial expectation used by vegan::specaccum(method=\"exact\"); no random subsampling is required. Returns a DataFrame with samples and richness columns. Empty samples contribute no new features.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.specaccum"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.taxonomy-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"taxonomy(c) -> Union{DataFrame,Nothing}\n\nTaxonomy, one row per feature, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.taxonomy"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.tiplabels-Tuple{CommunityEcology.PhyloTree}","page":"Home","text":"tiplabels(t) -> Vector{String}\n\nTip names of t, in tip order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.tiplabels"},{"category":"function","location":"#CommunityEcology.top_features","page":"Home","text":"top_features(c, n=10; by=:mean)\n\nNames of the n features with the highest mean relative abundance across samples (by=:mean), or the highest total count (by=:total).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.top_features"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.tree-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"tree(c) -> Union{PhyloTree,Nothing}\n\nPhylogenetic tree of the features, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.tree"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.unifrac-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"unifrac(c; weighted=false)\n\nUniFrac distances between the samples of c, using its tree. Unweighted: the fraction of branch length leading to features in one sample but not both. Weighted (normalised, as phyloseq::UniFrac(weighted=TRUE) and GUniFrac's d_1): branch lengths weighted by differences in relative abundance below them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.unifrac"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.varespec-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"varespec()\n\nCover of 44 lichen, moss and vascular plant species in 24 lichen pastures under Scots pine in eastern Fennoscandia (Väre, Ohtonen & Oksanen 1995), with soil chemistry as metadata: N, P, K, Ca, Mg, S, Al, Fe, Mn, Zn, Mo (mg/kg), Baresoil (% cover), Humdepth (humus depth, cm) and pH.\n\nThe same data as varespec and varechem in R's vegan package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.varespec"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.wisconsin-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"wisconsin(c)\n\nWisconsin double standardization: divide each feature by its maximum across samples, then divide each sample by its resulting total. All-zero features and samples remain zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.wisconsin"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.write_community-Tuple{AbstractString, Community}","page":"Home","text":"write_community(dir, c)\n\nSave c as plain files in the folder dir: counts.csv (one row per sample), taxonomy.csv and sequences.fasta (one entry per feature), metadata.csv and tree.nwk, when present. In R, read.csv(\"counts.csv\", row.names = 1) gives the matrix phyloseq's otu_table expects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.write_community"},{"category":"method","location":"#CommunityEcology.write_newick-Tuple{CommunityEcology.PhyloTree}","page":"Home","text":"write_newick([io or file,] t::PhyloTree)\n\nThe tree in Newick format (a string, or written to io or a file), for FigTree, iTOL, R's ape::read.tree or Julia's Phylo.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommunityEcology.write_newick"},{"category":"method","location":"#DataAPI.metadata-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"metadata(c) -> DataFrame\n\nSample metadata, one row per sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DataAPI.metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"#PERMANOVA.pairwise_permanova-Tuple{Community, Symbol}","page":"Home","text":"pairwise_permanova(c::Community, variable::Symbol; distance=:braycurtis, kw...)\n\nPERMANOVA between every pair of levels of the metadata column variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PERMANOVA.pairwise_permanova"},{"category":"method","location":"#PERMANOVA.permanova-Tuple{Community, StatsModels.FormulaTerm}","page":"Home","text":"permanova(c::Community, formula; distance=:braycurtis, kw...)\n\nPERMANOVA of the samples of c (dissimilarity distance, see beta_diversity) on its metadata. Keywords go to PERMANOVA.permanova: permutations, by, strata, rng.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PERMANOVA.permanova"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatsBase.counts-Tuple{Community}","page":"Home","text":"counts(c) -> Matrix\n\nSamples × features count matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsBase.counts"}]
}
